{"name":"Spruce Stone","hex":"#9fc09c","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spruce","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various large coniferous evergreen trees or shrubs from the genus Picea, found in northern temperate and boreal regions; originally and more fully spruce fir.","The wood of a spruce."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Smart, trim, and elegant in appearance; fastidious (said of a person)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spruce"},{"term":"stone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A hard earthen substance that can form rocks; especially, such substance when regarded as a building material.","A piece of such material: a block of stone, rock or a pebble."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Constructed of stone.","Having the appearance of stone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/stone"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spruce","blocks":[{"text":"A spruce is a tree of the genus Picea, a genus of some 37 species of coniferous trees in the pine family, Pinaceae, found in the northern temperate and boreal (taiga) regions of the Northern Hemisphere. Picea is treated either in the subfamily Pinoideae, or the sole genus in its own subfamily Piceoideae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Picea_abies.jpg/330px-Picea_abies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spruce"},{"title":"Rock (geology)","blocks":[{"text":"In geology, a rock is any naturally occurring solid mass or aggregate of minerals or mineraloid matter. It is categorized by the minerals included, its chemical composition, and the way in which it is formed. Rocks form the Earth's outer solid layer, the crust, and most of its interior, except for the liquid outer core and pockets of magma in the asthenosphere. The study of rocks involves multiple subdisciplines of geology, including petrology and mineralogy. It may be limited to rocks found on Earth, or it may include planetary geology that studies the rocks of other celestial objects."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg/330px-Marsh_Butte_and_Geikie_Peak%2C_Grand_Canyon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rock_(geology)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/495_Picea_abies_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-495_Picea_abies_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/495_Picea_abies_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-495_Picea_abies_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Picea abies anatomy. 1: young female cone; 2: male cones; 3: mature female cone; 4: pulvinus at leaf base; 5: squarish cross-section of leaf; 6: top of scale; 7: underside of scale; 10: winged seed","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A495_Picea_abies_(cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/2020_year._Herbarium._Picea_abies._img-007_%28cropped%29.jpg/250px-2020_year._Herbarium._Picea_abies._img-007_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/2020_year._Herbarium._Picea_abies._img-007_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-2020_year._Herbarium._Picea_abies._img-007_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/2020_year._Herbarium._Picea_abies._img-007_%28cropped%29.jpg/250px-2020_year._Herbarium._Picea_abies._img-007_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2020_year._Herbarium._Pi